const f="Anyone who contacts you out of the blue offering to recover your money for an upfront fee, especially if they claim to be from a regulator, police unit or “blockchain recovery” service, is very likely running a second scam.",D=[{slug:"investment-fraud",name:"Investment fraud",title:"Lost money to investment fraud? What to do first",description:"A practical guide for victims of investment fraud: the first steps to take, the evidence lawyers need, a realistic timeline and how no-win-no-fee recovery works.",intro:"Investment fraud covers Ponzi schemes, fake funds, unauthorised trading, mis-sold products and advisers who misrepresented risk. The steps you take in the first weeks often decide whether money can still be traced and recovered.",caseType:"investment-fraud",firstSteps:[{title:"Stop sending money",body:"Do not pay any further “fees”, “taxes” or “unlock charges” to withdraw funds. These requests are a hallmark of fraud and the money is almost never returned."},{title:"Secure your records",body:"Download account statements, contracts, emails, chat histories and website screenshots now, fraudulent platforms often disappear without warning."},{title:"Report it",body:"Report to the police and to the financial regulator in your country. Keep the reference numbers: lawyers and banks will ask for them."},{title:"Contact your bank",body:"Tell your bank or card issuer immediately. Depending on how you paid, there may be chargeback, recall or reimbursement routes with short time limits."},{title:"Get a recovery assessment",body:"A lawyer can assess whether the people or entities involved, or the bank, broker or adviser who facilitated it, can be pursued, and whether assets can be frozen."}],evidence:["Account statements and transaction history","Bank transfer confirmations with beneficiary details","Contracts, prospectuses, brochures or pitch decks","Emails, messages and call logs with the people involved","Website or app screenshots, including URLs","Police and regulator report references","Names of anyone who introduced you to the investment"],timeline:[{phase:"Days 1–14",detail:"Secure evidence, report, contact your bank. Urgent freezing applications, where justified, happen in this window."},{phase:"Weeks 2–8",detail:"A lawyer reviews the evidence, traces funds and identifies who can be pursued and where."},{phase:"Months 2–6",detail:"Pre-action letters, regulator complaints or ombudsman routes; negotiation with any regulated party involved."},{phase:"6 months +",detail:"Court proceedings or group action where needed. Duration varies widely by country and complexity."}],redFlags:[f,"Guaranteed returns or pressure to invest quickly were early warning signs, note when and how they were made, as they can matter as evidence."],faq:[{q:"Can I get my money back from an investment scam?",a:"Sometimes. It depends on whether the money can be traced, whether a solvent party (a bank, a regulated firm or an identifiable individual with assets) is responsible, and how quickly you act. A lawyer can tell you which of these applies to you."},{q:"Do lawyers take investment fraud cases on a no-win-no-fee basis?",a:"Many do, where recovery looks realistic, for example when a regulated entity or insurer is involved, or many investors were affected by the same scheme. Where it is less certain, firms may offer a reduced fee plus a success fee, or refer you to a litigation funder."},{q:"Should I join a group claim?",a:"Group or collective actions can share costs and make funding easier. Check who runs the claim, how fees are taken from any recovery, and whether you can leave."}]},{slug:"crypto-scam",name:"Crypto scam",title:"Crypto scam recovery: first steps and how lawyers trace funds",description:"What to do after a crypto scam: preserve wallet and transaction evidence, report it, avoid recovery scams and understand how lawyers trace and freeze crypto assets.",intro:"Crypto transfers are public on the blockchain, which means funds can sometimes be followed to an exchange where an account holder can be identified. Speed matters: once funds are moved through mixers or off-ramped, options narrow.",caseType:"crypto-fraud",firstSteps:[{title:"Record every transaction",body:"Write down each transfer: date, amount, coin, sending and receiving wallet addresses and the transaction hash (TXID)."},{title:"Notify the exchanges involved",body:"If you sent funds from an exchange account, or know which exchange received them, report the fraud to its compliance team with the transaction hashes."},{title:"Report to police",body:"Many countries have dedicated cybercrime units. A police report is often needed before exchanges or courts will act."},{title:"Beware of recovery scams",body:f},{title:"Speak to a specialist lawyer",body:"Lawyers who work with blockchain-analysis firms can tell you whether the funds reached a point where a disclosure or freezing order is realistic."}],evidence:["Transaction hashes (TXIDs) for every transfer","Your wallet addresses and the scammer's addresses","Exchange account statements and KYC details you used","Screenshots of the platform, app or website and its URL","Chats with the people involved (Telegram, WhatsApp, dating apps, email)",'Any "withdrawal fee" or "tax" demands you received',"Police report reference"],timeline:[{phase:"Hours to days",detail:"Notify exchanges and police; preserve evidence before accounts and sites vanish."},{phase:"Weeks 1–4",detail:"Blockchain tracing to see where funds went and whether an exchange holds them."},{phase:"Weeks 2–8",detail:"Where justified, court applications for disclosure or freezing orders against exchanges or unknown persons."},{phase:"Months +",detail:"Claims against identified individuals or platforms; recovery depends on what is frozen."}],redFlags:[f,"Nobody can honestly guarantee crypto recovery. Treat guarantees as a warning sign."],faq:[{q:"Can stolen crypto be recovered?",a:"Sometimes, if the funds can be traced to an exchange or custodian that can be compelled to freeze and disclose account information. If funds were moved through mixers or to wallets nobody controls identifiably, recovery is much harder."},{q:"Is there a no-win-no-fee option for crypto fraud?",a:"Some firms work on success terms when tracing shows funds at an identifiable exchange. Initial tracing reports often carry a cost, so ask upfront what is included."},{q:"How do I spot a recovery scam?",a:"Unsolicited contact, upfront fees, claims of special access to blockchains or regulators, and pressure to act fast. Legitimate law firms are regulated and can be checked with the local bar or law society."}]},{slug:"property-fraud",name:"Property fraud",title:"Property and off-plan fraud: how to protect your claim",description:"Guide for buyers hit by property fraud or failed off-plan developments: first steps, documents to gather, timelines and how no-win-no-fee lawyers assess these claims.",intro:"Property fraud ranges from fake listings and diverted deposits to off-plan developments that never complete and title problems discovered after purchase. Your contracts and payment records are the foundation of any claim.",caseType:"real-estate",firstSteps:[{title:"Gather the contract chain",body:"Collect the reservation agreement, purchase contract, any bank guarantee or insurance certificate, and all addenda."},{title:"Map every payment",body:"List each payment with date, amount, the account it went to and who received it. Payments to personal accounts rather than the developer's are an important detail."},{title:"Check the registry",body:"A land-registry or title search shows who owns the property and whether it is mortgaged or encumbered."},{title:"Talk to other buyers",body:"If a development has failed, other buyers are often in the same position; a group claim may be possible."},{title:"Get advice in the property's country",body:"Property claims are usually governed by the law of the country where the property is located, that is where you need a lawyer."}],evidence:["Reservation and purchase contracts","Bank guarantees, escrow or deposit-protection documents","Payment receipts and bank statements","Land-registry or title extracts","Marketing materials and promised completion dates","Correspondence with the developer, agent and notary"],timeline:[{phase:"Weeks 1–4",detail:"Document review, registry checks, identifying guarantees or insurers."},{phase:"Months 1–3",detail:"Formal demands, claims under guarantees or insurance, coordination with other buyers."},{phase:"Months 3 +",detail:"Court proceedings, insolvency claims or criminal complaints where relevant."}],redFlags:["Requests to pay deposits to a personal account or a different company than the one on the contract.","Pressure to sign quickly or without independent legal review."],faq:[{q:"Can I recover a deposit from a developer that went bust?",a:"Possibly, if your deposit was protected by a bank guarantee, escrow or insurance, or if directors can be held personally liable. Otherwise you may be an unsecured creditor in the insolvency."},{q:"Do I need a lawyer in the country where the property is?",a:"Usually yes, because property claims are generally governed by local law and heard by local courts."}]},{slug:"unpaid-invoices",name:"Unpaid invoices",title:"Client won't pay? A guide to recovering unpaid invoices",description:"Step-by-step guide to recovering unpaid invoices: reminders, formal demands, evidence to collect, timelines and success-fee debt recovery options.",intro:"Most unpaid invoices are resolved without court, but a structured approach, and acting before the debtor's position worsens, improves your chances.",caseType:"unpaid-invoice",firstSteps:[{title:"Check the paper trail",body:"Make sure you have the contract or order, the invoice and proof of delivery or completion."},{title:"Send a clear final reminder",body:"State the amount, due date and a short deadline, and that you will take formal action if unpaid."},{title:"Send a formal demand",body:"A letter before action from a lawyer often prompts payment and is required in some countries before suing."},{title:"Check the debtor's status",body:"A company-registry search shows whether the debtor is still trading or in insolvency, this changes the strategy."},{title:"Choose the route",body:"Small claims procedures, payment-order procedures or a full claim, depending on amount and whether the debt is disputed."}],evidence:["Contract, purchase order or written acceptance of your quote","Invoices and statement of account","Proof of delivery or sign-off","Reminders and any reply acknowledging the debt","Company-registry details for the debtor"],timeline:[{phase:"Week 1–2",detail:"Final reminder and formal demand."},{phase:"Weeks 2–8",detail:"Payment-order or small-claims filing for undisputed debts."},{phase:"Months +",detail:"Full proceedings if the debt is disputed, then enforcement."}],redFlags:["New complaints about quality only after you chase payment, keep evidence of earlier acceptance.","Debtor moving assets or setting up a new company."],faq:[{q:"Will a lawyer recover a debt on a no-win-no-fee basis?",a:"Many debt-recovery firms work for a percentage of what they collect, particularly for undisputed business debts."},{q:"Can I claim interest and costs?",a:"Many countries allow statutory interest and some recovery costs on late business payments. A lawyer can confirm what applies to your invoice."}]},{slug:"business-partner-fraud",name:"Business partner fraud",title:"When a business partner takes the money: your options",description:"Guide to disputes where a co-founder, partner or majority shareholder diverts funds or excludes you: first steps, evidence, timelines and funding options.",intro:"Partnership and shareholder disputes often start with exclusion from information and end with diverted revenue, dilution or asset stripping. Acting early protects both evidence and value.",caseType:"partnership-dispute",firstSteps:[{title:"Preserve access and evidence",body:"Save copies of accounts, bank statements, minutes and emails you are entitled to, before access is cut off. Do not take material you have no right to."},{title:"Read your agreements",body:"The shareholders' or partnership agreement and articles usually set out information rights, deadlock rules and exit mechanisms."},{title:"Request information formally",body:"Use your statutory and contractual rights to request accounts and records in writing."},{title:"Value your stake",body:"Understanding what your stake is worth tells you which remedies: buy-out, damages, or unwinding transactions, are worth pursuing."},{title:"Consider urgent relief",body:"If assets are being moved, a lawyer may be able to seek an injunction quickly."}],evidence:["Shareholders' or partnership agreement and articles","Cap table and share registers","Management accounts and bank statements","Board minutes and resolutions","Emails and messages showing the conduct","Evidence of related-party transactions"],timeline:[{phase:"Weeks 1–4",detail:"Information requests, evidence preservation, valuation."},{phase:"Months 1–3",detail:"Negotiation, mediation or demand for buy-out."},{phase:"Months 3 +",detail:"Court proceedings or arbitration under the agreement."}],redFlags:["Sudden new share issues or transfers to new companies.","Being removed as director or signatory without notice."],faq:[{q:"Can a shareholder dispute be funded?",a:"Yes, where the business or stake is valuable enough. Funders and success-fee firms look at what can realistically be recovered or bought out."}]},{slug:"romance-scam",name:"Romance scam",title:"Romance and pig-butchering scams: what victims can do",description:"Practical steps after a romance or 'pig-butchering' investment scam: preserving evidence, reporting, bank options, avoiding recovery scams and getting legal help.",intro:"Romance scams build trust over weeks or months, often before introducing an “investment” platform. Victims are not at fault, these are organised operations. There are practical steps that can still help.",caseType:"crypto-fraud",firstSteps:[{title:"Cut contact and stop paying",body:"Do not send more money, including “fees” to release funds."},{title:"Save everything",body:"Export chats and profiles, and save photos, usernames, phone numbers, wallet addresses and payment details."},{title:"Report to your bank, the platform and police",body:"Banks may have reimbursement routes for authorised push-payment fraud depending on the country; dating platforms can preserve account data."},{title:"Watch for recovery scams",body:f},{title:"Get an assessment",body:"A lawyer can tell you whether funds can be traced or whether a bank or platform has any responsibility."}],evidence:["Chat exports and profile screenshots","Payment records and wallet addresses",'Platform or app URLs used for the "investment"',"Police and bank report references"],timeline:[{phase:"Immediately",detail:"Bank and police reports; evidence preservation."},{phase:"Weeks",detail:"Tracing and bank reimbursement claims."},{phase:"Months",detail:"Legal claims where a recoverable party is identified."}],redFlags:[f],faq:[{q:"Will my bank refund a romance scam payment?",a:"It depends on your country, how you paid and the bank's rules. Some countries have reimbursement schemes for authorised push-payment fraud. Report it to the bank immediately."}]},{slug:"insurance-claim-denied",name:"Denied insurance claim",title:"Insurance claim denied? How to challenge it",description:"What to do when an insurer rejects, delays or under-pays your claim: reading the policy, internal complaints, ombudsman routes, evidence and success-fee lawyers.",intro:"Insurers reject claims for many reasons, exclusions, late notification, alleged non-disclosure or disputes about the value of the loss. Many rejections can be challenged.",caseType:"insurance-claims",firstSteps:[{title:"Get the reasons in writing",body:"Ask the insurer to identify the exact policy clauses it relies on."},{title:"Read the full policy",body:"Compare the wording, including definitions and exclusions, with the reasons given."},{title:"Use the complaints process",body:"Many countries require an internal complaint before an ombudsman or regulator will look at it."},{title:"Document the loss independently",body:"Get your own assessor, contractor or expert report if the insurer's valuation seems low."},{title:"Check deadlines",body:"Both the policy and the law may impose time limits on challenging a decision."}],evidence:["Full policy wording and schedule","Claim form and all correspondence","Rejection letter","Photos, invoices and reports on the loss","Loss adjuster's report"],timeline:[{phase:"Weeks 1–8",detail:"Internal complaint and response."},{phase:"Months 2–6",detail:"Ombudsman or regulator complaint where available."},{phase:"Months +",detail:"Court proceedings for larger or disputed claims."}],redFlags:["Signing a settlement or release before getting advice.","Missing a policy deadline for disputing the decision."],faq:[{q:"Do lawyers take insurance disputes on a no-win-no-fee basis?",a:"Often, because the insurer can pay. The main question is whether the policy wording supports your claim."}]},{slug:"professional-negligence",name:"Professional negligence",title:"Bad advice from a professional? Claiming for negligence",description:"How to approach a claim against a lawyer, accountant, surveyor or adviser whose error cost you money: first steps, evidence, timelines and funding.",intro:"Professionals owe a duty to act with reasonable skill and care. When they fall short and it causes a financial loss, you may have a claim, usually against their professional indemnity insurance.",caseType:"professional-negligence",firstSteps:[{title:"Request your file",body:"Ask for a copy of your complete file and engagement terms."},{title:"Write down the timeline",body:"When you instructed them, what advice was given, and when you discovered the problem."},{title:"Quantify the loss",body:"What would your position have been if the advice or work had been correct?"},{title:"Check deadlines",body:"Limitation periods for negligence can run from when the loss occurred or when you discovered it, and may be short."},{title:"Get an independent opinion",body:"A specialist will usually need an expert in the same profession to confirm the standard was not met."}],evidence:["Engagement letter and terms","The advice, report or work product","Correspondence","Documents showing the financial consequence"],timeline:[{phase:"Weeks 1–6",detail:"File review and initial expert view."},{phase:"Months 1–4",detail:"Letter of claim and response from the professional's insurer."},{phase:"Months +",detail:"Negotiation, mediation or proceedings."}],redFlags:["Letting the professional “fix it” without independent advice while time runs."],faq:[{q:"Who pays if I win a professional negligence claim?",a:"Usually the professional's indemnity insurer, which is why these claims are often funded on success terms."}]}],i="Under EU Regulation 261/2004 (and its UK copy, UK261), passengers can claim fixed compensation for long delays at arrival, late cancellations and denied boarding, unless the airline shows the disruption was caused by extraordinary circumstances such as severe weather or air traffic control restrictions. The amount depends on the flight distance.",r="When a flight is cancelled, passengers are entitled to choose between a refund and re-routing, and to care (meals, and a hotel if an overnight stay is needed) while they wait. Customers have brought complaints where refunds were slow or only vouchers were offered.",l="Lost, delayed or damaged luggage is covered by the Montreal Convention, which has short notice periods: report it at the airport and send a written claim to the airline quickly.",c=["There is no single EU deadline for flight compensation: the limitation period is set by the law of the country where you bring the claim, and it varies considerably between countries.","Baggage claims under the Montreal Convention have much shorter notice periods (days, not years) and a two-year limit for court action.","Use the limitation checker for an indicative deadline in your country, and act early: evidence such as boarding passes and emails is easier to gather now."],d=(e=!0)=>[{to:"/tools/flight-compensation",label:"Flight compensation calculator (EU261 and UK261)"},{to:"/tools/claim-letter",label:"Free complaint letter template"},{to:"/tools/limitation-checker",label:"Limitation deadline checker"},{to:"/lawyers/consumer",label:"Consumer claim lawyers by country"},{to:"/free-alternatives",label:"Free complaint routes before hiring a lawyer"},...e?[{to:"/es/guias/vuelo-retrasado-o-cancelado",label:"Vuelo retrasado o cancelado (guía en español)",lang:"es"}]:[]],u=["Booking confirmation and booking reference","Boarding passes (or proof of check-in)","Emails, texts or app notifications about the delay or cancellation","Photos of departure boards showing times","Receipts for meals, hotels and transport you paid for","The airline's reply to your complaint"],h=[{phase:"Day of travel",detail:"Keep boarding passes, ask the airline staff for the reason for the delay in writing, keep receipts."},{phase:"Weeks 1–6",detail:"Claim directly with the airline through its online form. Many claims are settled at this stage."},{phase:"Weeks 6 +",detail:"If refused or ignored, escalate to the national enforcement body or an approved alternative dispute resolution scheme, or to court."},{phase:"Months +",detail:"Court proceedings, often through a simplified small-claims procedure. Duration varies by country."}],m=["Claim companies and law firms typically keep a share of the compensation. Compare that with claiming directly, which is free.","An airline voucher is not the same as a refund. You can usually insist on money back for a cancelled flight."],p=e=>[{q:`Do I need a lawyer to claim compensation from ${e}?`,a:"Usually not for the first step: you can claim directly with the airline for free. A claim company or no-win-no-fee lawyer can help if the airline refuses or does not reply, in exchange for a share of the compensation."},{q:`Does ${e} have to pay if the delay was caused by weather or strikes?`,a:"Not if the disruption was caused by extraordinary circumstances that could not have been avoided with reasonable measures, such as severe weather. Courts have held that strikes by the airline's own staff are not normally extraordinary, while strikes by third parties such as air traffic controllers can be. Each case depends on its facts."},{q:"How much compensation can I get?",a:"Under EU261 the fixed amounts are 250, 400 or 600 euros depending on distance (UK261 uses pounds). Our flight compensation calculator shows which band applies. Refunds and expenses for care are separate from compensation."}],w=["Read any settlement offer from the bank carefully before you sign. Some agreements include a waiver of further claims.","Check that a claims firm is a regulated law firm or authorised claims manager, and get the fee in writing before you sign."],v=[{phase:"Weeks 1–4",detail:"Gather the contract, statements and any later agreements. Send a written complaint to the bank's customer service."},{phase:"Months 1–3",detail:"Wait for the bank's reply. If it refuses or does not answer, escalate to the free ombudsman or supervisor complaints service."},{phase:"Months 3 +",detail:"Court proceedings where needed. Many consumer banking cases in Spain are decided on documents, but timing varies widely between courts."}],k=["Loan or mortgage deed, card or product contract","Statements or amortisation schedule showing what you paid","Any later agreements or settlement documents you signed","Marketing material or information you were given before signing","Your written complaint and the bank's reply"],C=["In Spain, the Supreme Court and the Court of Justice of the European Union (CJEU) have held that the action to declare an unfair term void in a consumer contract does not expire. The time limit to recover money already paid is more debated: the CJEU has said it cannot start running before the consumer could know the term was unfair.","Deadlines differ by product and are still developing through case law, so do not wait: get documents together and take advice early."],g=[{to:"/es/guias/clausula-suelo",label:"Cláusula suelo (guía en español)",lang:"es"},{to:"/es/guias/gastos-hipoteca",label:"Gastos de hipoteca (guía en español)",lang:"es"},{to:"/es/guias/tarjetas-revolving",label:"Tarjetas revolving (guía en español)",lang:"es"},{to:"/es/guias/irph",label:"IRPH (guía en español)",lang:"es"},{to:"/free-alternatives",label:"Free complaint routes, including the Banco de España"},{to:"/lawyers/consumer/spain",label:"Consumer claim lawyers in Spain"},{to:"/tools/limitation-checker/spain",label:"Limitation checker for Spain"}],A=[{title:"Floor clauses (cláusulas suelo)",body:"Mortgage terms that set a minimum interest rate. Since 2013 Spanish courts have held them void where they were not transparent, and since the CJEU's December 2016 ruling banks must repay everything charged under a void clause, with interest."},{title:"Mortgage set-up costs (gastos de hipoteca)",body:"Customers have claimed back part of the notary, registry, agency and valuation costs charged when the mortgage was signed, after courts held clauses passing all costs to the customer to be unfair."},{title:"Revolving credit cards",body:"Claims that the interest rate was usurious or that the cost of the card was not explained clearly. The Supreme Court has ruled on how to compare revolving rates with the market average."},{title:"IRPH-indexed mortgages",body:"Claims about mortgages indexed to IRPH instead of Euribor, where the question is whether the bank explained the index and its effect transparently."}],S=e=>[{q:`Do I have to complain to ${e} before going to court?`,a:"In practice, yes. A written complaint to the bank's customer service is required before you can go to the Banco de España complaints service, and since April 2025 most civil claims in Spain require a prior attempt to settle. A documented written complaint usually serves both purposes."},{q:"Do lawyers take these cases on a no-win-no-fee basis?",a:"Many do, especially floor clause and mortgage expense claims, because when the bank loses in full it is normally ordered to pay costs. Ask for the fee agreement (hoja de encargo) in writing, including what happens if the bank settles early."},{q:"Can I still claim if I have paid off the loan?",a:"Often yes. Having repaid or cancelled a mortgage does not by itself prevent a claim about an unfair term, but any later agreement or waiver you signed needs to be reviewed."}],I=["Purchase or lease contract and invoice","Vehicle registration document and VIN","Engine type and emissions class","Any recall or software update letters","Finance agreement, if the car was financed","Mileage at purchase and today"],U=[{phase:"Weeks 1–4",detail:"Identify the engine and whether it was subject to a recall; collect purchase and finance documents."},{phase:"Months 1–3",detail:"Check whether a group action or collective claim covers your vehicle, and its deadlines to join."},{phase:"Months 3 +",detail:"Individual court proceedings or participation in a group claim. Group cases can take several years."}],B=["Deadlines to join group actions can close long before the general limitation period. Check them early.","Read how a claims firm or funder is paid: many take a percentage of any compensation and some charge fees if you withdraw."],T=["Limitation periods depend on the country, and the start date often depends on when buyers knew or should have known about the issue. Many claims about vehicles affected in 2015 have reached or passed the ordinary limit in some countries.","Group actions have their own registration or opt-in deadlines. Use the limitation checker and ask a lawyer before assuming your claim is still open."],x=[{to:"/es/guias/dieselgate",label:"Dieselgate en España (guía en español)",lang:"es"},{to:"/de/ratgeber/dieselskandal",label:"Dieselskandal (Ratgeber auf Deutsch)",lang:"de"},{to:"/lawyers/class-actions",label:"Class action and group claim lawyers"},{to:"/guides/class-actions-vs-eu-representative-actions",label:"US class actions vs EU representative actions"},{to:"/tools/limitation-checker",label:"Limitation deadline checker"}],P=[{slug:"ryanair",name:"Ryanair",title:"Claims against Ryanair: delays, cancellations and refunds",description:"How to claim against Ryanair for delayed or cancelled flights under EU261 and UK261, refunds and baggage: steps, evidence, deadlines and when a lawyer helps.",intro:"Ryanair is an Irish airline, so its flights from or to the EU are generally covered by EU Regulation 261/2004, and flights from the UK by UK261. Most passenger claims against it are about long delays, cancellations, refunds and baggage. This guide explains how those claims usually work.",caseType:"consumer",company:{sector:"Airline",claims:[{title:"Delay and cancellation compensation",body:i},{title:"Refunds and care",body:r},{title:"Baggage",body:l},{title:"Extra charges",body:"Some passengers have brought complaints about fees for bags, seats or check-in. These are usually treated as consumer complaints rather than EU261 claims."}],deadlines:c,links:d()},firstSteps:[{title:"Claim directly with the airline",body:"Use Ryanair's online claim form for EU261 compensation and refunds. Keep the claim reference number and screenshots."},{title:"Note the actual arrival time",body:"Compensation for delay depends on arrival time at the destination (when a door opens), not take-off time. Write it down or keep app notifications."},{title:"Keep receipts",body:"Expenses for meals, hotels and transport during the disruption can be claimed in addition to compensation."},{title:"Escalate if refused",body:"If the airline refuses or does not reply, you can go to the national enforcement body of the country where the incident happened, an approved dispute resolution scheme where one exists, or court."},{title:"Consider help on success terms",body:"Claim companies and no-win-no-fee lawyers can take over refused claims for a share of the compensation."}],evidence:u,timeline:h,redFlags:m,faq:p("Ryanair")},{slug:"vueling",name:"Vueling",title:"Claims against Vueling: delays, cancellations and refunds",description:"How to claim against Vueling for delayed or cancelled flights under EU261, refunds and baggage: steps, evidence, deadlines and the role of AESA in Spain.",intro:"Vueling is a Spanish airline, so its flights are generally covered by EU Regulation 261/2004. Passenger claims against it are mostly about long delays, cancellations, missed connections, refunds and baggage. In Spain, the aviation safety agency AESA handles passenger complaints.",caseType:"consumer",company:{sector:"Airline",claims:[{title:"Delay and cancellation compensation",body:i},{title:"Refunds and care",body:r},{title:"Baggage",body:l}],deadlines:c,links:[...d(),{to:"https://www.seguridadaerea.gob.es/",label:"AESA, Spanish aviation safety agency (official site)"}]},firstSteps:[{title:"Claim directly with the airline",body:"Submit a claim through Vueling's customer service channels with your booking reference and a clear request: compensation, refund or expenses."},{title:"Note the actual arrival time",body:"Delay compensation depends on how late you arrived at the final destination, not how late the flight left."},{title:"Keep receipts",body:"Meals, hotels and transport during the disruption can be claimed in addition to compensation."},{title:"Escalate to AESA or court",body:"If the airline refuses or does not reply, you can complain to AESA for incidents in Spain, or go to court. In Spain, claims up to 2,000 euros can be brought in a juicio verbal without a lawyer."},{title:"Consider help on success terms",body:"Claim companies and no-win-no-fee lawyers can take over refused claims for a share of the compensation."}],evidence:u,timeline:h,redFlags:m,faq:p("Vueling")},{slug:"easyjet",name:"easyJet",title:"Claims against easyJet: delays, cancellations and refunds",description:"How to claim against easyJet for delayed or cancelled flights under EU261 or UK261, refunds and baggage: steps, evidence, deadlines and escalation.",intro:"easyJet operates through a UK company and separate European companies, so a flight can fall under the EU rules (EU261) or the UK rules (UK261) depending on the route and the operating carrier. Passenger claims are mostly about long delays, cancellations, refunds and baggage.",caseType:"consumer",company:{sector:"Airline",claims:[{title:"Delay and cancellation compensation",body:i},{title:"EU261 or UK261?",body:"Flights departing from the UK are covered by UK261; flights departing from the EU, or arriving in the EU on an EU-licensed airline, by EU261. Your boarding pass or booking shows which easyJet company operated the flight."},{title:"Refunds and care",body:r},{title:"Baggage",body:l}],deadlines:c,links:d()},firstSteps:[{title:"Claim directly with the airline",body:"Use easyJet's online claim form and keep the claim reference."},{title:"Check which rules apply",body:"Note the departure airport and the operating company: this decides whether EU261 or UK261 applies, and the currency of compensation."},{title:"Keep receipts",body:"Expenses for meals, hotels and transport during the disruption can be claimed in addition to compensation."},{title:"Escalate if refused",body:"For UK flights, airlines are usually members of an approved alternative dispute resolution scheme; otherwise the Civil Aviation Authority can help. For EU flights, use the national enforcement body or court."},{title:"Consider help on success terms",body:"Claim companies and no-win-no-fee lawyers can take over refused claims for a share of the compensation."}],evidence:u,timeline:h,redFlags:m,faq:p("easyJet")},{slug:"lufthansa",name:"Lufthansa",title:"Claims against Lufthansa: delays, cancellations and refunds",description:"How to claim against Lufthansa for delayed or cancelled flights under EU261, refunds and baggage: steps, evidence, deadlines and German conciliation.",intro:"Lufthansa is a German airline, so its flights are generally covered by EU Regulation 261/2004, including long-haul flights departing from the EU. Passenger claims are mostly about long delays, cancellations, missed connections, refunds and baggage. In Germany, independent conciliation bodies handle passenger disputes free of charge for passengers.",caseType:"consumer",company:{sector:"Airline",claims:[{title:"Delay and cancellation compensation",body:i},{title:"Missed connections",body:"When connecting flights are booked together and you reach the final destination more than three hours late, compensation is generally calculated on the distance to the final destination."},{title:"Refunds and care",body:r},{title:"Baggage",body:l}],deadlines:[...c.slice(0,2),"In Germany, the regular limitation period is three years from the end of the year in which the claim arose. Use the limitation checker for other countries."],links:[...d(!1),{to:"/de/ratgeber/flugverspaetung",label:"Flugverspätung (Ratgeber auf Deutsch)",lang:"de"},{to:"https://soep-online.de/",label:"söp conciliation body (official site, German)"}]},firstSteps:[{title:"Claim directly with the airline",body:"Use Lufthansa's online feedback and claim forms with your booking reference and a clear request."},{title:"Note the actual arrival time",body:"Compensation for delay depends on arrival time at the final destination."},{title:"Keep receipts",body:"Expenses for meals, hotels and transport during the disruption can be claimed in addition to compensation."},{title:"Use conciliation if refused",body:"In Germany, airline passenger disputes can go to the söp conciliation body for member airlines, or to the federal air transport conciliation body at the Federal Office of Justice. Both are free for passengers."},{title:"Consider help on success terms",body:"Passenger rights portals and lawyers can take over refused claims, usually for a share of the compensation."}],evidence:u,timeline:h,redFlags:m,faq:p("Lufthansa")},{slug:"iberia",name:"Iberia",title:"Claims against Iberia: delays, cancellations and refunds",description:"How passengers claim against Iberia for delayed or cancelled flights under EU261, refunds and baggage: steps, evidence, deadlines and escalation in Spain.",intro:"Iberia is a Spanish airline, so its flights are generally covered by EU Regulation 261/2004, including long-haul flights departing from Spain. Passenger claims are mostly about long delays, cancellations, missed connections, refunds and baggage.",caseType:"consumer",company:{sector:"Airline",claims:[{title:"Delay and cancellation compensation",body:i},{title:"Missed connections",body:"When connecting flights are booked together and you reach the final destination more than three hours late, compensation is generally calculated on the distance to the final destination."},{title:"Refunds and care",body:r},{title:"Baggage",body:l}],deadlines:c,links:[...d(),{to:"https://www.seguridadaerea.gob.es/",label:"AESA, Spanish aviation safety agency (official site)"}]},firstSteps:[{title:"Claim directly with the airline",body:"Submit a claim through Iberia's customer service channels with your booking reference and a clear request."},{title:"Note the actual arrival time",body:"Compensation for delay depends on arrival time at the final destination."},{title:"Keep receipts",body:"Expenses for meals, hotels and transport during the disruption can be claimed in addition to compensation."},{title:"Escalate to AESA or court",body:"If the airline refuses or does not reply, complain to AESA for incidents in Spain, or go to court. In Spain, claims up to 2,000 euros can be brought in a juicio verbal without a lawyer."},{title:"Consider help on success terms",body:"Claim companies and no-win-no-fee lawyers can take over refused claims for a share of the compensation."}],evidence:u,timeline:h,redFlags:m,faq:p("Iberia")},{slug:"santander",name:"Santander",title:"Claims against Santander: mortgages, cards and Banco Popular",description:"What customers claim against Santander in Spain (floor clauses, mortgage costs, revolving cards, Banco Popular), how to complain and when a lawyer helps.",intro:"Banco Santander is one of Spain's largest banks and acquired Banco Popular in 2017. Like other Spanish banks, it has faced consumer claims over mortgage terms and credit products, and former Banco Popular investors brought claims after Popular's 2017 resolution. This guide explains the usual claims and routes in Spain.",caseType:"consumer",company:{sector:"Bank",claims:[...A,{title:"Banco Popular shares and bonds",body:"Banco Popular was resolved by the European authorities in June 2017 and sold to Santander. Shareholders and bondholders who lost their investment have brought claims, mainly arguing that they were misinformed when they bought. Outcomes have depended on when and how the investment was made."}],deadlines:C,links:[...g,{to:"/es/guias/preferentes-y-subordinadas",label:"Preferentes y subordinadas (guía en español)",lang:"es"}]},firstSteps:[{title:"Find your documents",body:"Locate the mortgage deed, card contract or investment orders, plus statements and any later agreements with the bank."},{title:"Complain in writing to the bank",body:"Send a written complaint to Santander's customer service (Servicio de Atención al Cliente), explaining what you claim and why. Keep proof of submission."},{title:"Escalate for free",body:"If the bank refuses or does not reply in time, you can go to the Banco de España complaints service (banking) or the CNMV (investments). Their reports are not binding on the bank."},{title:"Take advice before court",body:"A lawyer can check whether the clause or product is challengeable, the deadline, and whether a prior settlement or waiver affects your claim."}],evidence:k,timeline:v,redFlags:w,faq:S("Santander")},{slug:"bbva",name:"BBVA",title:"Claims against BBVA: floor clauses, mortgage costs and cards",description:"What customers typically claim against BBVA in Spain (floor clauses, mortgage costs, revolving cards, IRPH), how to complain, deadlines and when a lawyer helps.",intro:"BBVA is one of Spain's largest banks. It was among the banks in the case in which the Spanish Supreme Court first ruled on floor clauses in May 2013, and like other Spanish banks it has faced consumer claims over mortgage terms and credit products. This guide explains the usual claims and routes in Spain.",caseType:"consumer",company:{sector:"Bank",claims:A,deadlines:C,links:g},firstSteps:[{title:"Find your documents",body:"Locate the mortgage deed or card contract, statements or the amortisation schedule, and any later agreements with the bank."},{title:"Complain in writing to the bank",body:"Send a written complaint to BBVA's customer service (Servicio de Atención al Cliente), explaining what you claim and why. Keep proof of submission."},{title:"Escalate for free",body:"If the bank refuses or does not reply in time, you can go to the Banco de España complaints service. Its reports are not binding on the bank."},{title:"Take advice before court",body:"A lawyer can check whether the clause is challengeable, the deadline, and whether any settlement or waiver affects your claim."}],evidence:k,timeline:v,redFlags:w,faq:S("BBVA")},{slug:"caixabank",name:"CaixaBank",title:"Claims against CaixaBank: mortgages, cards and former Bankia",description:"What customers claim against CaixaBank in Spain (floor clauses, mortgage costs, revolving cards, former Bankia), how to complain and when a lawyer helps.",intro:"CaixaBank is one of Spain's largest banks and absorbed Bankia in 2021, so it is now the counterparty for many claims that began against Bankia. Like other Spanish banks, it has faced consumer claims over mortgage terms and credit products. This guide explains the usual claims and routes in Spain.",caseType:"consumer",company:{sector:"Bank",claims:[...A,{title:"Former Bankia products",body:"Retail investors brought claims over Bankia's 2011 stock market listing, and in 2016 the Supreme Court allowed retail buyers to annul their purchases because of serious inaccuracies in the prospectus. Holders of preference shares (preferentes) and subordinated debt sold by Bankia and the savings banks that formed it also brought mis-selling claims."}],deadlines:C,links:[...g,{to:"/es/guias/preferentes-y-subordinadas",label:"Preferentes y subordinadas (guía en español)",lang:"es"}]},firstSteps:[{title:"Find your documents",body:"Locate the mortgage deed, card contract or investment orders, statements and any later agreements with the bank or with Bankia."},{title:"Complain in writing to the bank",body:"Send a written complaint to CaixaBank's customer service (Servicio de Atención al Cliente). Claims about former Bankia products now go to CaixaBank."},{title:"Escalate for free",body:"If the bank refuses or does not reply in time, you can go to the Banco de España complaints service (banking) or the CNMV (investments). Their reports are not binding on the bank."},{title:"Take advice before court",body:"Investment claims from many years ago raise difficult deadline questions; a lawyer can tell you whether yours is still open."}],evidence:k,timeline:v,redFlags:w,faq:S("CaixaBank")},{slug:"volkswagen",name:"Volkswagen",title:"Claims against Volkswagen: the diesel emissions scandal",description:"How owners have claimed against Volkswagen over diesel emissions (dieselgate): who could claim, group actions, deadlines and how no-win-no-fee lawyers work.",intro:"In September 2015 Volkswagen admitted that software in some of its diesel engines, mainly the EA189 engine used across the VW, Audi, SEAT and Skoda brands, made the cars perform differently in emissions tests than on the road. Owners in many countries have since brought individual and group claims. This guide explains how those claims have generally worked.",caseType:"class-actions",company:{sector:"Carmaker",claims:[{title:"Damages for buyers",body:"Owners have claimed the car was worth less than they paid, or that they would not have bought it had they known. In Germany, the Federal Court of Justice (BGH) ruled in May 2020 that buyers of cars with the EA189 engine could claim damages against Volkswagen, with a deduction for the use they had of the car."},{title:"Group and collective actions",body:"Consumer associations and claimant groups have run collective cases in several countries, including a model declaratory action in Germany that ended in a settlement in 2020, and group litigation in England and Wales that settled in 2022 without an admission of liability."},{title:"Other engines and brands",body:"Claims have also been brought about other diesel engines and brands in the group. Whether a particular car is affected depends on the engine and model year."}],deadlines:T,links:x},firstSteps:[{title:"Identify your engine",body:"Check the engine code and whether your car received a recall or software update letter. Manufacturers and national type-approval authorities have published vehicle checkers."},{title:"Check for group actions",body:"Ask consumer associations and law firms in your country whether a collective case covers your car, and whether registration is still open."},{title:"Gather documents",body:"Purchase or lease contract, finance agreement, registration documents and mileage records."},{title:"Get a deadline check",body:"Limitation is often the main issue in older diesel cases. A lawyer can tell you quickly whether your claim is still open."}],evidence:I,timeline:U,redFlags:B,faq:[{q:"Can I still claim against Volkswagen for dieselgate?",a:"It depends on the country, the engine, when you bought the car and whether you joined a group action. In some countries ordinary deadlines for EA189 cars have passed; in others, or for other engines, claims may still be possible. Get a deadline check before anything else."},{q:"Do I need to still own the car?",a:"Not necessarily. Courts in some countries have allowed claims by buyers who have since sold the car, with the sale price taken into account."},{q:"Do lawyers take diesel claims on a no-win-no-fee basis?",a:"Many do, often with litigation funding or legal expenses insurance, because claims are numerous and similar. Read how the fee is calculated and what happens if you withdraw."}]},{slug:"mercedes-benz",name:"Mercedes-Benz",title:"Claims against Mercedes-Benz: diesel emissions",description:"How owners have claimed against Mercedes-Benz over diesel emissions: recalls, the 2023 CJEU ruling, group claims, deadlines and no-win-no-fee options.",intro:"Owners of some Mercedes-Benz diesel cars have brought claims arguing that emissions controls worked differently on the road than in tests, for example through temperature-dependent settings. German authorities ordered recalls of some models, and the manufacturer has disputed that its vehicles contained unlawful defeat devices. This guide explains how these claims have generally worked.",caseType:"class-actions",company:{sector:"Carmaker",claims:[{title:"Damages for buyers in the EU",body:"In March 2023 the Court of Justice of the EU ruled (case C-100/21, against Mercedes-Benz Group) that buyers can claim damages from a manufacturer that negligently installed an unlawful defeat device. Following that ruling, the German Federal Court of Justice (BGH) allowed damages for negligence, generally between 5% and 15% of the purchase price, depending on the case."},{title:"Group claims in England and Wales",body:"Group claims over diesel emissions involving several manufacturers, Mercedes-Benz among them, have been brought in the High Court in London."},{title:"Which cars are affected",body:"Claims depend on the specific engine, model and emissions class. A recall or software update letter is a useful starting point, but not every recalled car gives rise to a claim, and vice versa."}],deadlines:T,links:x},firstSteps:[{title:"Identify your engine",body:"Check the engine code, the emissions class and whether your car received a recall or software update letter."},{title:"Check for group claims",body:"Ask law firms and consumer associations in your country whether a group case covers your model, and whether registration is still open."},{title:"Gather documents",body:"Purchase or lease contract, finance agreement, registration documents and mileage records."},{title:"Get a deadline check",body:"Limitation depends on the country and on when buyers could know about the issue. A lawyer can tell you quickly whether your claim is still open."}],evidence:I,timeline:U,redFlags:B,faq:[{q:"Is my Mercedes affected?",a:"It depends on the engine, model and emissions class. Recall letters and official vehicle checkers are a starting point; a lawyer or claims group can check your specific car."},{q:"How much could I get?",a:"There is no fixed amount. In Germany, courts following the 2023 CJEU ruling have generally awarded a percentage of the purchase price for negligence claims; elsewhere it depends on national law and the claim."},{q:"Do lawyers take these cases on a no-win-no-fee basis?",a:"Many do, often backed by litigation funding or legal expenses insurance. Ask how the fee is calculated and what happens if the claim fails."}]}],a={eu261:{href:"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2004/261/oj",label:"Regulation (EC) No 261/2004 (EUR-Lex)"},uk261:{href:"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/eur/2004/261/contents",label:"UK261: Regulation 261/2004 as retained in UK law (legislation.gov.uk)"},rail:{href:"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2021/782/oj",label:"Regulation (EU) 2021/782 on rail passengers' rights (EUR-Lex)"},floor:{href:"https://curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-154/15",label:"CJEU, Gutiérrez Naranjo, joined cases C-154/15, C-307/15 and C-308/15"},irph:{href:"https://curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-125/18",label:"CJEU, Gómez del Moral Guasch, case C-125/18 (IRPH)"},costs:{href:"https://curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-224/19",label:"CJEU, CaixaBank and BBVA, cases C-224/19 and C-259/19 (mortgage costs)"},bde:{href:"https://clientebancario.bde.es/",label:"Banco de España: Portal del Cliente Bancario"},chf1:{href:"https://curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-260/18",label:"CJEU, Dziubak, case C-260/18"},chf2:{href:"https://curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-520/21",label:"CJEU, Bank M., case C-520/21"},rf:{href:"https://rf.gov.pl/",label:"Rzecznik Finansowy (Polish Financial Ombudsman)"},trucks:{href:"https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/detail/en/IP_16_2582",label:"European Commission: trucks cartel decision, July 2016"},scania:{href:"https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/detail/en/IP_17_3021",label:"European Commission: Scania decision, September 2017"},trucksCjeu:{href:"https://curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-267/20",label:"CJEU, Volvo and DAF Trucks, case C-267/20 (limitation of cartel claims)"},damages:{href:"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2014/104/oj",label:"Directive 2014/104/EU on competition damages actions (EUR-Lex)"},shopping:{href:"https://curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-48/22",label:"CJEU, Google and Alphabet v Commission, case C-48/22 P (Google Shopping)"},booking:{href:"https://curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-264/23",label:"CJEU, Booking.com, case C-264/23 (parity clauses)"},vw:{href:"https://juris.bundesgerichtshof.de/cgi-bin/rechtsprechung/document.py?Gericht=bgh&Art=en&az=VI%20ZR%20252/19",label:"Federal Court of Justice (BGH), VI ZR 252/19, 25 May 2020"},mercedes:{href:"https://curia.europa.eu/juris/liste.jsf?num=C-100/21",label:"CJEU, Mercedes-Benz Group, case C-100/21"}},q={ryanair:{country:"ireland",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.eu261,a.uk261]},vueling:{country:"spain",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.eu261]},easyjet:{country:"united-kingdom",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.uk261,a.eu261]},lufthansa:{country:"germany",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.eu261]},iberia:{country:"spain",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.eu261]},santander:{country:"spain",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.floor,a.costs,a.bde]},bbva:{country:"spain",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.floor,a.costs,a.bde]},caixabank:{country:"spain",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.floor,a.costs,a.bde]},volkswagen:{country:"germany",deadlineBasis:"tort",sources:[a.vw]},"mercedes-benz":{country:"germany",deadlineBasis:"tort",sources:[a.mercedes]}},t=e=>{const n=e.uk&&e.eu!==!1?"EU261 and UK261":e.uk?"UK261":"EU261";return{slug:e.slug,name:e.name,title:`Claims against ${e.name}: delays, cancellations and refunds`,description:`How to claim against ${e.name} for a delayed or cancelled flight under ${n}: refunds, baggage, where to complain and deadlines.`,intro:e.intro,caseType:"consumer",companies:[e.name],country:e.country,deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:e.uk&&e.eu===!1?[a.uk261]:e.uk?[a.eu261,a.uk261]:[a.eu261],company:{sector:"Airline",claims:[{title:"Delay and cancellation compensation",body:i},{title:"Refunds and care",body:r},{title:"Baggage",body:l},...e.extra||[]],deadlines:c,links:[...d(!1),...e.guideLinks||[]]},firstSteps:[{title:`Claim directly with ${e.name}`,body:`Use ${e.name}'s own claim or customer relations form, with your booking reference, the flight number and what you ask for (compensation, refund or expenses). Keep the reference number.`},{title:"Write down the arrival time",body:"Compensation for delay depends on when the aircraft door opened at the final destination, not on the take-off time. Keep app notifications or take a photo of the arrivals board."},{title:"Keep every receipt",body:"Meals, hotel nights and transport you paid for because of the disruption are claimed on top of any fixed compensation."},{title:"Escalate if refused",body:e.escalate},{title:"Consider help on success terms",body:"Claim companies and no-win-no-fee lawyers take over refused claims for a share of the compensation. Compare that share with claiming yourself, which costs nothing."}],evidence:u,timeline:h,redFlags:m,faq:p(e.name)}},M="If the airline refuses or does not answer, complain to the national enforcement body of the country where the flight departed (or arrived, for flights into the EU), use an approved dispute resolution scheme where the airline takes part in one, or go to court, usually through a small-claims procedure.",N=[t({slug:"air-france",name:"Air France",country:"france",intro:"Air France is France's flag carrier and part of the Air France-KLM group. As an EU airline, its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU and also when they fly into the EU from elsewhere. Claims are mostly about long delays, cancellations, missed connections on long-haul trips and refunds.",escalate:"If Air France refuses or does not answer, you can complain to the DGAC (Direction générale de l'aviation civile) for incidents in France, go to the Médiation Tourisme et Voyage, or bring a claim in court. In other countries, use that country's enforcement body.",guideLinks:[{to:"/fr/guides/vol-retarde-annule",label:"Vol retardé ou annulé (guide en français)",lang:"fr"}]}),t({slug:"klm",name:"KLM",country:"netherlands",intro:"KLM Royal Dutch Airlines is the Dutch airline of the Air France-KLM group, with its hub at Amsterdam Schiphol. As an EU airline, its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU and when they fly into the EU. Many claims concern connections through Amsterdam, where the delay at the final destination is what counts.",escalate:"If KLM refuses or does not answer, you can complain to the Human Environment and Transport Inspectorate (ILT) for flights from the Netherlands, to the enforcement body of the departure country for other flights, or go to court."}),t({slug:"british-airways",name:"British Airways",country:"united-kingdom",uk:!0,intro:"British Airways is the UK's flag carrier and part of International Airlines Group (IAG). Flights from the UK, and BA flights into the UK, are covered by UK261, the UK copy of the EU rules with amounts in pounds; BA flights departing from the EU are covered by EU261. Claims are mostly about delays, cancellations, refunds and baggage.",escalate:"If British Airways refuses or does not answer, you can use an approved alternative dispute resolution scheme, complain to the Civil Aviation Authority (CAA), or make a court claim for money, which in England and Wales can be done online."}),t({slug:"wizz-air",name:"Wizz Air",uk:!0,intro:"Wizz Air is a low-cost airline headquartered in Hungary, with a separate UK company for many flights from the UK. Its flights from the EU are covered by EU261 and flights operated by its UK company from or to the UK by UK261. Claims are mostly about cancellations, long delays, refunds and extra charges.",escalate:M}),t({slug:"tap-air-portugal",name:"TAP Air Portugal",country:"portugal",intro:"TAP Air Portugal is Portugal's flag carrier, with its hub in Lisbon and many routes to Brazil and Africa. As an EU airline, its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU and when they fly into the EU, which includes flights from Brazil to Lisbon.",escalate:"If TAP refuses or does not answer, you can complain to Portugal's civil aviation authority (ANAC) for incidents in Portugal, to the enforcement body of the departure country for other flights, or go to court."}),t({slug:"ita-airways",name:"ITA Airways",country:"italy",intro:"ITA Airways is the Italian airline that started flying in October 2021, when Alitalia stopped operating. It is a separate company from Alitalia, so claims about earlier Alitalia flights are not normally brought against ITA. Its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from or fly into the EU.",escalate:"If ITA Airways refuses or does not answer, you can complain to ENAC, Italy's civil aviation authority, for incidents in Italy, to the enforcement body of the departure country for other flights, or go to court.",guideLinks:[{to:"/it/guide/volo-in-ritardo-cancellato",label:"Volo in ritardo o cancellato (guida in italiano)",lang:"it"}]}),t({slug:"aer-lingus",name:"Aer Lingus",country:"ireland",intro:"Aer Lingus is an Irish airline and part of International Airlines Group (IAG). Its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU or fly into the EU, and flights from the UK by UK261. Claims are mostly about cancellations, delays on transatlantic routes, refunds and baggage.",uk:!0,escalate:M}),t({slug:"eurowings",name:"Eurowings",country:"germany",intro:"Eurowings is the low-cost airline of the Lufthansa Group, based in Germany. Its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU or fly into the EU. Claims are mostly about delays, cancellations, refunds and baggage on short-haul European routes.",escalate:"If Eurowings refuses or does not answer, you can go to the transport conciliation body söp, complain to the Federal Aviation Office (Luftfahrt-Bundesamt) for incidents in Germany, or go to court.",guideLinks:[{to:"/de/ratgeber/flugverspaetung",label:"Flugverspätung (Ratgeber auf Deutsch)",lang:"de"}]}),t({slug:"swiss",name:"SWISS",country:"switzerland",intro:"Swiss International Air Lines (SWISS) is Switzerland's national airline and part of the Lufthansa Group. Switzerland applies Regulation 261/2004 under its air transport agreement with the EU, so SWISS flights from Swiss and EU airports are covered in the same way as an EU airline's.",escalate:"If SWISS refuses or does not answer, you can complain to the Federal Office of Civil Aviation (FOCA) for incidents in Switzerland, to the enforcement body of the departure country for flights from the EU, or go to court."}),t({slug:"austrian-airlines",name:"Austrian Airlines",country:"austria",intro:"Austrian Airlines is Austria's national airline and part of the Lufthansa Group, with its hub in Vienna. Its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU or fly into the EU. Claims are mostly about delays, missed connections through Vienna, cancellations and refunds.",escalate:"If Austrian Airlines refuses or does not answer, you can go to the apf (Agentur für Passagier- und Fahrgastrechte), Austria's free conciliation body for passengers, or go to court."}),t({slug:"brussels-airlines",name:"Brussels Airlines",country:"belgium",intro:"Brussels Airlines is Belgium's main airline and part of the Lufthansa Group, with its hub at Brussels Airport and many routes to Africa. Its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU or fly into the EU.",escalate:"If Brussels Airlines refuses or does not answer, you can complain to the Belgian Federal Public Service Mobility and Transport for incidents in Belgium, to the enforcement body of the departure country for other flights, or go to court."}),t({slug:"volotea",name:"Volotea",country:"spain",intro:"Volotea is a low-cost airline registered in Spain that mainly connects medium-sized European cities. As an EU airline, its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004. Claims are mostly about cancellations, schedule changes, long delays and refunds.",escalate:"If Volotea refuses or does not answer, you can complain to AESA, Spain's aviation safety agency, for incidents in Spain, to the enforcement body of the departure country for other flights, or go to court. In Spain, claims up to 2,000 euros can be brought without a lawyer.",guideLinks:[{to:"/es/guias/vuelo-retrasado-o-cancelado",label:"Vuelo retrasado o cancelado (guía en español)",lang:"es"}]}),t({slug:"air-europa",name:"Air Europa",country:"spain",intro:"Air Europa is a Spanish airline based in Mallorca, with a hub in Madrid and many routes to Latin America. As an EU airline, its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU and when they fly into the EU, including flights from Latin America to Madrid.",escalate:"If Air Europa refuses or does not answer, you can complain to AESA, Spain's aviation safety agency, for incidents in Spain, to the enforcement body of the departure country for other flights, or go to court. In Spain, claims up to 2,000 euros can be brought without a lawyer.",guideLinks:[{to:"/es/guias/vuelo-retrasado-o-cancelado",label:"Vuelo retrasado o cancelado (guía en español)",lang:"es"}]}),t({slug:"norwegian",name:"Norwegian",country:"norway",intro:"Norwegian Air Shuttle is a Norwegian low-cost airline. Norway applies Regulation 261/2004 as part of the European Economic Area, so Norwegian's flights are covered in the same way as an EU airline's. Claims are mostly about delays, cancellations and refunds.",escalate:"If Norwegian refuses or does not answer, passengers in Norway can go to the Transport Complaints Board (Transportklagenemnda); for flights from other countries, use the enforcement body of the departure country, or go to court."}),t({slug:"transavia",name:"Transavia",country:"netherlands",intro:"Transavia is the low-cost airline of the Air France-KLM group, with a Dutch company and a French company (Transavia France). Its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004. Which company operated your flight decides where you complain, so check the flight number and your booking.",escalate:"If Transavia refuses or does not answer, you can complain to the Human Environment and Transport Inspectorate (ILT) for flights from the Netherlands, to the DGAC for flights from France, or go to court."}),t({slug:"jet2",name:"Jet2",country:"united-kingdom",uk:!0,intro:"Jet2.com is a UK leisure airline based in Leeds, often sold with Jet2holidays packages. Its flights from the UK are covered by UK261 and its flights from EU airports back to the UK by EU261. Package holidaymakers also have rights against the tour operator.",escalate:"If Jet2 refuses or does not answer, you can use an approved alternative dispute resolution scheme, complain to the Civil Aviation Authority (CAA), or make a court claim for money."}),t({slug:"sas",name:"SAS",country:"sweden",intro:"SAS (Scandinavian Airlines) is the airline of Denmark, Norway and Sweden, headquartered near Stockholm. Its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU or EEA, or fly into it. Claims are mostly about delays, cancellations, refunds and baggage.",escalate:"If SAS refuses or does not answer, passengers in Sweden can go to the National Board for Consumer Disputes (ARN); elsewhere, use the enforcement body of the departure country, or go to court."}),t({slug:"lot-polish-airlines",name:"LOT Polish Airlines",country:"poland",intro:"LOT Polish Airlines is Poland's flag carrier, with its hub in Warsaw. As an EU airline, its flights are covered by Regulation 261/2004 when they depart from the EU and when they fly into the EU, including long-haul flights to Warsaw.",escalate:"If LOT refuses or does not answer, you can complain to Poland's Civil Aviation Authority (ULC) for incidents in Poland, to the enforcement body of the departure country for other flights, or go to court.",guideLinks:[{to:"/pl/poradniki/opozniony-lot",label:"Opóźniony lot (poradnik po polsku)",lang:"pl"}]}),t({slug:"turkish-airlines",name:"Turkish Airlines",country:"turkey",intro:"Turkish Airlines is Türkiye's flag carrier, with its hub in Istanbul. Because it is not an EU airline, Regulation 261/2004 covers its flights departing from an EU airport, but not its flights from Istanbul into the EU. Türkiye has its own air passenger rules for flights from Turkish airports.",escalate:"For a flight that departed from the EU, complain to the enforcement body of the departure country if Turkish Airlines refuses or does not answer, or go to court there. For flights from Türkiye, Turkish rules and authorities apply."})],o=e=>({slug:e.slug,name:e.name,title:`Claims against ${e.name}: floor clauses, mortgage costs and cards`,description:`What customers claim against ${e.name} in Spain (floor clauses, mortgage costs, IRPH, cards), the free complaint route and deadlines.`,intro:e.intro,caseType:"consumer",companies:[e.name],country:"spain",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.floor,a.irph,a.costs,a.bde],company:{sector:"Bank",claims:[...A,...e.extra||[]],deadlines:C,links:e.preferentes?[...g,{to:"/es/guias/preferentes-y-subordinadas",label:"Preferentes y subordinadas (guía en español)",lang:"es"}]:g},firstSteps:[{title:"Find your documents",body:"Locate the mortgage deed, card contract or investment orders, statements or the amortisation schedule, and any later agreement you signed with the bank."},{title:`Complain in writing to ${e.name}`,body:`Send a written complaint to ${e.name}'s customer service (Servicio de Atención al Cliente) saying what you claim and why, and keep proof of submission.`},{title:"Escalate for free",body:"If the bank refuses or does not reply in time, you can go to the Banco de España complaints service (banking) or the CNMV (investments). Their reports are not binding on the bank."},{title:"Take advice before court",body:"A lawyer can check whether the clause or product can be challenged, the deadline, and whether an earlier settlement or waiver affects your claim."}],evidence:k,timeline:v,redFlags:w,faq:S(e.name)}),j=[o({slug:"bankinter",name:"Bankinter",intro:"Bankinter is a Spanish bank known for its mortgage and private banking business. Besides the mortgage terms common to Spanish banks, customers have brought claims over multi-currency mortgages (hipotecas multidivisa) linked to foreign currencies such as the Swiss franc or the yen. This guide explains the usual claims and routes in Spain.",extra:[{title:"Multi-currency mortgages",body:"Mortgages repaid in or indexed to a foreign currency. Customers have claimed that the currency risk was not explained clearly; Spanish courts have annulled the currency clause where the bank could not show it gave enough information."}]}),o({slug:"banco-sabadell",name:"Banco Sabadell",preferentes:!0,intro:"Banco Sabadell is one of Spain's largest banks and took over Caja de Ahorros del Mediterráneo (CAM) in 2012, so it now answers many claims about CAM mortgages and products. Like other Spanish banks, it has faced consumer claims over mortgage terms and credit products.",extra:[{title:"Former CAM products",body:"Holders of CAM preference shares (cuotas participativas, preferentes) and subordinated debt brought mis-selling claims after the savings bank was rescued. Outcomes depended on how and when the product was sold."}]}),o({slug:"unicaja",name:"Unicaja",intro:"Unicaja is a Spanish bank from Málaga that merged with Liberbank in 2021, after earlier absorbing Caja España-Duero. Customers of all these banks now bring their claims against Unicaja, mostly about mortgage terms and credit products."}),o({slug:"ibercaja",name:"Ibercaja",intro:"Ibercaja is a Spanish bank based in Zaragoza, created from a savings bank, which absorbed Caja3 in 2013. Like other Spanish banks, it has faced consumer claims over floor clauses, mortgage costs, IRPH-indexed mortgages and credit cards."}),o({slug:"abanca",name:"Abanca",preferentes:!0,intro:"Abanca is a Galician bank formed from the savings banks Caixa Galicia and Caixanova (later NCG Banco), which was among the banks in the case where the Spanish Supreme Court first ruled on floor clauses in May 2013. It now answers claims about NCG and the former savings banks' products.",extra:[{title:"Former NCG preference shares",body:"Customers who bought preference shares (preferentes) and subordinated debt from the Galician savings banks brought mis-selling claims, and many were offered arbitration or settlements. Check what you signed before starting a new claim."}]}),o({slug:"kutxabank",name:"Kutxabank",intro:"Kutxabank is a Basque bank formed from the savings banks BBK, Kutxa and Caja Vital, and it owns Cajasur in Andalusia. Customers of these banks have brought consumer claims over floor clauses, mortgage costs, IRPH-indexed mortgages and credit products."})],K=[{title:"Swiss franc mortgages (kredyty frankowe)",body:"Borrowers with mortgages indexed to or denominated in Swiss francs have claimed that the exchange-rate clauses were unfair because the bank set the rate and did not explain the currency risk. In Dziubak (C-260/18, October 2019) the Court of Justice of the EU held that a court may annul the whole contract where it cannot continue without the unfair term."},{title:"What happens if the contract is annulled",body:"Both sides return what they received: the borrower the capital, the bank the instalments. In June 2023 (C-520/21) the CJEU held that EU law precludes the bank from claiming remuneration beyond the capital it lent, and Polish courts have since applied that ruling."},{title:"Settlements offered by banks",body:"Several banks have offered settlements converting the loan into zlotys. A settlement can end the dispute, so compare it with the likely result of a court case before signing."}],y=e=>({slug:e.slug,name:e.name,title:`Claims against ${e.name}: Swiss franc mortgages`,description:`How borrowers claim against ${e.name} over Swiss franc mortgages: the CJEU rulings, settlements, the Financial Ombudsman and deadlines.`,intro:e.intro,caseType:"consumer",companies:[e.name],country:"poland",deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.chf1,a.chf2,a.rf],company:{sector:"Bank",claims:K,deadlines:["In Poland, claims by consumers are generally subject to a six-year limitation period that ends on the last day of the calendar year. For Swiss franc loans, courts have held that the period for the borrower runs from when the borrower knew, or should have known, that the clause was unfair.","Deadlines in these cases are still argued in court, so do not wait: gather the loan documents and get advice early."],links:[{to:"/pl/poradniki/kredyty-frankowe",label:"Kredyty frankowe (poradnik po polsku)",lang:"pl"},{to:"/lawyers/consumer/poland",label:"Consumer claim lawyers in Poland"},{to:"/tools/limitation-checker/poland",label:"Limitation checker for Poland"},{to:"/free-alternatives",label:"Free complaint routes before hiring a lawyer"}]},firstSteps:[{title:"Gather the loan documents",body:"The loan agreement and annexes, the bank's tables of exchange rates if you have them, and a history of repayments (the bank must provide one on request)."},{title:`Ask ${e.name} for the repayment history`,body:"A certificate of the instalments paid in zlotys and francs is the basis of any calculation of what you could recover."},{title:"Consider the Financial Ombudsman",body:"The Rzecznik Finansowy handles complaints against financial institutions free of charge and can support a borrower in court."},{title:"Compare any settlement offer",body:"Before accepting a bank's conversion offer, ask a lawyer to compare it with the likely outcome of annulling the contract."}],evidence:["Loan agreement, annexes and the bank's rules (regulamin)","Certificate of repayments in zlotys and francs","Correspondence with the bank and any settlement offer","Your written complaint and the bank's reply"],timeline:[{phase:"Weeks 1–4",detail:"Gather documents, ask the bank for the repayment certificate, send a written complaint."},{phase:"Months 1–3",detail:"Review any settlement offer; consider the Financial Ombudsman or mediation."},{phase:"Months 3 +",detail:"Court proceedings. Many courts allow the borrower to stop paying instalments while the case runs (an interim measure); ask your lawyer."}],redFlags:["Read how a law firm is paid: a success fee as a percentage of the recovered amount can be large in these cases.","A settlement normally ends all claims about the loan, so compare it before signing."],faq:[{q:`Can I still sue ${e.name} if I have repaid the Swiss franc loan?`,a:"Often yes. Borrowers who have repaid their loan have brought claims to recover what they overpaid, but the deadline question needs checking for your dates."},{q:"Do lawyers take these cases on a success fee?",a:"Many Polish firms offer a fixed fee plus a success fee, or a success fee only. Ask for the agreement in writing and how the fee is calculated if the bank settles."},{q:"What did the CJEU decide about bank remuneration?",a:"In June 2023 (case C-520/21) the Court held that, when a loan contract is annulled because of unfair terms, EU law precludes the bank from claiming compensation beyond the return of the capital and interest on it from when the payment was demanded."}]}),W=[y({slug:"pko-bp",name:"PKO Bank Polski",intro:"PKO Bank Polski is Poland's largest bank. It is one of the banks that hold Swiss franc mortgage portfolios, and many borrowers have brought claims about the exchange-rate clauses in those loans. This guide explains how those claims generally work."}),y({slug:"mbank",name:"mBank",intro:"mBank is a Polish bank majority-owned by Germany's Commerzbank. It sold mortgages indexed to the Swiss franc before the 2008 financial crisis and has faced a large number of claims from borrowers about the exchange-rate clauses."}),y({slug:"bank-millennium",name:"Bank Millennium",intro:"Bank Millennium is a Polish bank majority-owned by Portugal's Millennium bcp. It sold Swiss franc indexed mortgages and has faced many borrower claims about the exchange-rate clauses, and it has offered settlements to convert loans into zlotys."}),y({slug:"santander-bank-polska",name:"Santander Bank Polska",intro:"Santander Bank Polska, formerly Bank Zachodni WBK, is part of the Santander group. Borrowers with Swiss franc mortgages from the bank and from banks it took over have brought claims about the exchange-rate clauses."})],z=[{title:"Damages for overcharges",body:"In July 2016 the European Commission found that MAN, Volvo/Renault, Daimler, Iveco and DAF had colluded for 14 years (1997 to 2011) on truck pricing and on passing on the costs of emissions technology, and in September 2017 it found the same about Scania. Buyers and lessees of medium and heavy trucks from that period have claimed the extra price they paid."},{title:"Group claims",body:"Many hauliers have joined group claims organised by law firms, associations or claim vehicles, often backed by litigation funders, in Spain, Germany, the Netherlands, the United Kingdom and elsewhere."},{title:"How the overcharge is proved",body:"Claims rely on expert economic reports comparing cartel prices with the prices that would have applied without the cartel. Courts in several countries have estimated the damage when the evidence was incomplete."}],J=["In Volvo and DAF Trucks (C-267/20, June 2022) the CJEU held that the limitation period for these claims could not start before the Commission's decision was published in the EU Official Journal (April 2017 for the main decision), and applied the rules of the Damages Directive to many claims.","Each country's limitation rules then apply, and some claims are already out of time. Check your dates with a lawyer before assuming your claim is open."],s=e=>({slug:e.slug,name:e.name,title:`Claims against ${e.name}: the trucks cartel`,description:`How truck buyers claim damages from ${e.name} for the 1997 to 2011 trucks cartel: the Commission decision, group claims and deadlines.`,intro:e.intro,caseType:"class-actions",companies:[e.name],country:e.country,deadlineBasis:"tort",sources:e.sources||[a.trucks,a.trucksCjeu,a.damages],company:{sector:"Truck maker",claims:z,deadlines:J,links:[{to:"/es/guias/cartel-de-camiones",label:"Cártel de camiones (guía en español)",lang:"es"},{to:"/lawyers/class-actions",label:"Class action and group claim lawyers"},{to:"/funders",label:"Litigation funders directory"},{to:"/guides/class-actions-vs-eu-representative-actions",label:"US class actions vs EU representative actions"}]},firstSteps:[{title:"List your trucks",body:"Every medium or heavy truck bought, leased or financed between 1997 and 2011 (and in some claims, up to a few years after), with dates, model and price."},{title:"Find the invoices",body:"Purchase or leasing invoices, finance agreements and fleet registers. Where invoices are lost, registration records can help reconstruct the purchase."},{title:"Check whether you already joined a claim",body:"Many companies were approached by several claim organisers. Check that you have not assigned the same trucks twice."},{title:"Compare funding terms",body:"Group claims are usually funded: compare the share of any recovery that goes to the funder and lawyers, and what happens if you leave the group."}],evidence:["Purchase, leasing or finance invoices for each truck","Vehicle registration documents and fleet lists","Any resale invoices","Earlier agreements with claim organisers"],timeline:[{phase:"Weeks 1–4",detail:"List the trucks and gather invoices; check deadlines in your country."},{phase:"Months 1–6",detail:"Expert report on the overcharge; join a group claim or file an individual claim."},{phase:"1–4 years",detail:"Court proceedings. Many first-instance courts have ruled; appeals can take longer."}],redFlags:["Signing with more than one claim organiser for the same trucks.","Funding terms that keep a large share of the recovery even if you withdraw."],faq:[{q:`Can my company still claim against ${e.name}?`,a:"Possibly, depending on the country where you sue and when your limitation period started. After the CJEU's 2022 ruling many claims were filed, but each country's own rules decide whether yours is still open."},{q:"Do I need the original invoices?",a:"They help a lot. Where they are lost, registration records and bank statements can sometimes prove the purchase and its price."},{q:"Who pays the expert report?",a:"In group claims the funder or the law firm usually advances it and recovers it from the proceeds. In an individual claim, ask the firm how it is paid."}]}),V=[s({slug:"daimler-truck",name:"Daimler Truck",country:"germany",intro:"Daimler Truck, the German maker of Mercedes-Benz trucks, was one of the manufacturers fined by the European Commission in July 2016 for the trucks cartel. Buyers of its trucks from 1997 to 2011 have claimed damages for the overcharge, alone or in group claims."}),s({slug:"man-truck",name:"MAN Truck & Bus",country:"germany",intro:"MAN, the German truck maker in the Traton group, took part in the trucks cartel but was not fined because it revealed the cartel to the European Commission. That immunity does not protect it from damages claims: buyers of MAN trucks have also claimed for the overcharge."}),s({slug:"volvo-trucks",name:"Volvo Trucks",country:"sweden",intro:"The Swedish Volvo group, which also owns Renault Trucks, was fined by the European Commission in July 2016 for the trucks cartel. Buyers of Volvo and Renault trucks from 1997 to 2011 have claimed damages, and the CJEU's 2022 ruling on limitation came from a Spanish case against Volvo and DAF."}),s({slug:"daf-trucks",name:"DAF Trucks",country:"netherlands",intro:"DAF, the Dutch truck maker owned by PACCAR, was fined by the European Commission in July 2016 for the trucks cartel. DAF buyers have brought many of the leading cases, including the Spanish case that reached the CJEU in 2022."}),s({slug:"iveco",name:"Iveco",country:"italy",intro:"Iveco, the Italian truck maker, was fined by the European Commission in July 2016 for the trucks cartel. Buyers of Iveco trucks from 1997 to 2011 have claimed damages for the overcharge in Italy, Spain and other countries."}),s({slug:"scania",name:"Scania",country:"sweden",intro:"Scania, the Swedish truck maker in the Traton group, did not settle with the European Commission and was fined separately in September 2017 for the same cartel. Its appeals were dismissed, and buyers of Scania trucks have claimed damages for the overcharge.",sources:[a.scania,a.trucksCjeu,a.damages]})],O="Under Regulation (EU) 2021/782, which applies since June 2023, passengers can claim 25% of the ticket price for a delay at arrival of 60 to 119 minutes and 50% for 120 minutes or more, unless the delay was caused by extraordinary circumstances such as severe weather. Operators can set a minimum amount below which nothing is paid.",b=e=>({slug:e.slug,name:e.name,title:`Claims against ${e.name}: train delays and refunds`,description:`How to claim from ${e.name} for a delayed or cancelled train under EU rail passenger rights: amounts, refunds, complaints and deadlines.`,intro:e.intro,caseType:"consumer",companies:[e.name],country:e.country,deadlineBasis:"contract",sources:[a.rail],company:{sector:"Rail operator",claims:[{title:"Delay compensation",body:O},{title:"Refund or re-routing",body:"If a delay of 60 minutes or more is expected, passengers can choose a refund, continuing the journey or re-routing, and are entitled to meals and, where needed, a hotel."},...e.extra||[]],deadlines:["Regulation 2021/782 lets operators set a time limit for compensation requests, which cannot be shorter than three months after the journey. Ask for compensation as soon as possible.","A court claim after that is subject to the general limitation rules of the country where you sue."],links:[{to:"/tools/train-delay-compensation",label:"Train delay compensation calculator"},{to:"/tools/claim-letter",label:"Free complaint letter template"},{to:"/free-alternatives",label:"Free complaint routes before hiring a lawyer"},...e.lang?[e.lang]:[]]},firstSteps:[{title:`Claim with ${e.name}`,body:`Use ${e.name}'s compensation form with your ticket and the actual arrival time. Keep the reference.`},{title:"Keep your ticket and times",body:"A photo of the ticket and of the arrivals board, or the app's journey history, is usually enough evidence."},{title:"Escalate if refused",body:e.escalate}],evidence:["Ticket or booking confirmation","Planned and actual arrival times (photos, app history)","Receipts for meals, hotel or alternative transport","The operator's reply"],timeline:[{phase:"Day of travel",detail:"Note the arrival time and keep receipts."},{phase:"Weeks 1–4",detail:"Claim with the operator; most claims are paid at this stage."},{phase:"Months 1–3",detail:"If refused, go to the conciliation or enforcement body, or court."}],redFlags:["Small amounts rarely justify a lawyer: claim yourself first, it is free."],faq:[{q:`How much does ${e.name} pay for a delay?`,a:"Under the EU minimum rules, 25% of the ticket price for 60 to 119 minutes late and 50% for 120 minutes or more. Some operators pay more under their own commitments; check the conditions of your ticket."},{q:"Is a strike an extraordinary circumstance?",a:"Regulation 2021/782 lists exceptions such as extreme weather and major natural disasters; strikes by the operator's own staff are not among them. Check the facts of your case."}]}),H=[b({slug:"deutsche-bahn",name:"Deutsche Bahn",country:"germany",intro:"Deutsche Bahn is Germany's national rail operator. Its long-distance and regional trains are covered by the EU rail passenger rights rules, and German law adds some rights for local transport. Claims are mostly about delays, missed connections and cancelled trains.",escalate:"If Deutsche Bahn refuses or does not answer, you can go to the transport conciliation body söp, free for passengers, complain to the Federal Railway Authority (Eisenbahn-Bundesamt), or go to court.",lang:{to:"/de/werkzeuge/zugverspaetung",label:"Zugverspätung: Entschädigung berechnen (auf Deutsch)",lang:"de"}}),b({slug:"sncf",name:"SNCF Voyageurs",country:"france",intro:"SNCF Voyageurs runs France's TGV INOUI, Ouigo, Intercités and regional trains. Its services are covered by the EU rail passenger rights rules, and SNCF also has its own delay guarantees on some services. Claims are mostly about delays, cancellations and strikes.",escalate:"If SNCF refuses or does not answer, you can refer the dispute to the SNCF Voyageurs mediator (Médiateur SNCF Voyageurs), free for passengers, or go to court."}),b({slug:"renfe",name:"Renfe",country:"spain",intro:"Renfe is Spain's national rail operator, running AVE high-speed, long-distance and commuter trains. Its services are covered by the EU rail passenger rights rules, and Renfe also publishes its own punctuality commitment, which on some services is more generous than the EU minimum.",escalate:"If Renfe refuses or does not answer, you can go to your regional consumer arbitration board (Junta Arbitral de Consumo) where Renfe accepts it, complain to the consumer authorities, or go to court.",lang:{to:"/es/herramientas/indemnizacion-retraso-tren",label:"Indemnización por retraso de tren (en español)",lang:"es"}}),b({slug:"trenitalia",name:"Trenitalia",country:"italy",intro:"Trenitalia is Italy's main rail operator, running Frecce high-speed, Intercity and regional trains. Its services are covered by the EU rail passenger rights rules. Claims are mostly about delays, cancellations and missed connections.",escalate:"If Trenitalia refuses or does not answer, you can use its conciliation procedure, complain to the Transport Regulation Authority (ART), or go to court."})],L=["These are complex competition claims: check the claim organiser's funding terms and what you give up by joining."],_=[{slug:"google",name:"Google",title:"Claims against Google: competition damages (Google Shopping)",description:"How businesses claim damages from Google after the EU competition decisions on Google Shopping and Android: who can claim, evidence, deadlines and funding.",intro:"In June 2017 the European Commission fined Google for favouring its own comparison shopping service in search results. The General Court upheld the decision in 2021 and the Court of Justice of the EU dismissed Google's final appeal in September 2024 (case C-48/22 P). Price comparison sites and other businesses have since claimed damages in national courts.",caseType:"class-actions",companies:["Google"],deadlineBasis:"tort",sources:[a.shopping,a.damages],company:{sector:"Online platform",claims:[{title:"Comparison shopping services",body:"Businesses that ran comparison shopping sites in Europe have claimed lost profits caused by Google's conduct. A final infringement decision binds national courts, so these follow-on claims focus on the damage and its amount."},{title:"Advertisers and other businesses",body:"Group claims have also been brought on behalf of advertisers and other business users, arguing that Google's conduct raised prices. Whether they succeed depends on proving the harm."}],deadlines:["Under the Damages Directive the limitation period is at least five years and cannot start before the infringement ended and the claimant knew of it; national rules on when it starts differ.","The final CJEU judgment of September 2024 is relevant to when time runs in many countries, so check the dates with a lawyer."],links:[{to:"/lawyers/class-actions",label:"Class action and group claim lawyers"},{to:"/funders",label:"Litigation funders directory"},{to:"/guides/class-actions-vs-eu-representative-actions",label:"US class actions vs EU representative actions"}]},firstSteps:[{title:"Show how your business was affected",body:"Traffic, revenue and advertising data for the years of the infringement, and how they compare with competitors or later years."},{title:"Get an economic assessment",body:"These claims depend on an expert estimate of the lost profit or overcharge."},{title:"Compare group claims and funding",body:"Several funded group claims exist; compare their terms with an individual claim."}],evidence:["Traffic and revenue data for the relevant years","Advertising spend with Google","Business plans or forecasts from the time","Correspondence with Google about rankings"],timeline:[{phase:"Months 1–6",detail:"Data gathering and economic report."},{phase:"Years",detail:"Competition damages cases often take several years, including disclosure and expert evidence."}],redFlags:L,faq:[{q:"Do I have to prove that Google broke competition law?",a:"Not for the conduct covered by a final Commission decision: national courts are bound by it. You still have to prove the damage you suffered and its link to the infringement."},{q:"Are these claims funded?",a:"Often. Litigation funders back many competition damages claims in exchange for a share of the recovery."}]},{slug:"booking-com",name:"Booking.com",title:"Claims against Booking.com: parity clauses and hotel claims",description:"How hotels claim against Booking.com over price parity clauses after the CJEU's 2024 ruling: who can claim, evidence, deadlines, group claims and funding.",intro:"Booking.com, based in Amsterdam, used price parity clauses that stopped hotels offering lower prices elsewhere. In September 2024 the Court of Justice of the EU ruled (case C-264/23) that such clauses are not, in principle, an ancillary restraint exempt from competition law. Hotels in several countries have since brought or announced damages claims.",caseType:"class-actions",companies:["Booking.com"],country:"netherlands",deadlineBasis:"tort",sources:[a.booking,a.damages],company:{sector:"Online platform",claims:[{title:"Hotel damages claims",body:"Hotels have claimed that parity clauses kept commissions and room prices higher than they would otherwise have been. The CJEU ruling answered questions referred by a Dutch court in a dispute brought by German hotels."},{title:"Group claims",body:"Associations and funded claim vehicles have organised group claims for hotels in several European countries. Check their terms and the period they cover."}],deadlines:["Limitation depends on the country where the claim is brought and on when the hotel knew of the infringement. Clauses differed by country and period (wide and narrow parity), so dates matter.","Get the dates of your contract and of any clause changes checked early."],links:[{to:"/lawyers/class-actions/netherlands",label:"Class action lawyers in the Netherlands"},{to:"/funders",label:"Litigation funders directory"}]},firstSteps:[{title:"Collect your contracts",body:"Your agreements with Booking.com and the general terms in force each year, including the parity clauses."},{title:"Collect commission and price data",body:"Commission invoices and your own room prices on each channel over the years."},{title:"Compare group claim terms",body:"Check the share of any recovery the funder keeps and which years the claim covers."}],evidence:["Contracts and general terms by year","Commission invoices","Room price history on each channel","Correspondence about parity"],timeline:[{phase:"Months 1–6",detail:"Data gathering, economic analysis, choice between a group or individual claim."},{phase:"Years",detail:"Competition damages cases often take several years."}],redFlags:L,faq:[{q:"Can guests claim against Booking.com for parity clauses?",a:"The claims brought so far are by hotels, which paid the commissions. Guests' claims would be much harder to prove."},{q:"Does the CJEU ruling decide the damages?",a:"No. It answered questions of competition law; national courts decide whether there was an infringement in each case and how much damage it caused."}]}],$=P.map(e=>({...e,companies:e.companies??[e.name],...q[e.slug]||{}})),G=[...$,...N,...j,...W,...V,...H,..._],Y=["Airline","Rail operator","Bank","Carmaker","Truck maker","Online platform"],F=[...D,...G],Z=G.map(e=>e.slug),E=e=>F.find(n=>n.slug===e),R={"crypto-fraud":"crypto-scam","crypto-scams":"crypto-scam",crypto:"crypto-scam","cryptocurrency-scam":"crypto-scam","cryptocurrency-fraud":"crypto-scam","crypto-frauds":"crypto-scam","investment-frauds":"investment-fraud","investment-scam":"investment-fraud","investment-scams":"investment-fraud","property-frauds":"property-fraud","real-estate-fraud":"property-fraud","property-scam":"property-fraud","unpaid-invoice":"unpaid-invoices","debt-recovery":"unpaid-invoices","unpaid-debts":"unpaid-invoices","business-partner-frauds":"business-partner-fraud","partner-fraud":"business-partner-fraud","partnership-dispute":"business-partner-fraud","partnership-disputes":"business-partner-fraud","romance-scams":"romance-scam","romance-fraud":"romance-scam","dating-scam":"romance-scam","insurance-claim-denial":"insurance-claim-denied","insurance-claims":"insurance-claim-denied","insurance-claim":"insurance-claim-denied","denied-insurance-claim":"insurance-claim-denied","professional-negligence-claims":"professional-negligence","professional-negligence-claim":"professional-negligence",mercedes:"mercedes-benz",vw:"volkswagen","easy-jet":"easyjet","banco-santander":"santander","caixa-bank":"caixabank",airfrance:"air-france",ba:"british-airways",wizzair:"wizz-air",wizz:"wizz-air",tap:"tap-air-portugal",ita:"ita-airways",aerlingus:"aer-lingus","swiss-international-air-lines":"swiss",austrian:"austrian-airlines",lot:"lot-polish-airlines",turkish:"turkish-airlines",jet2com:"jet2",sabadell:"banco-sabadell",pko:"pko-bp","pko-bank-polski":"pko-bp",millennium:"bank-millennium",daimler:"daimler-truck",man:"man-truck",volvo:"volvo-trucks","renault-trucks":"volvo-trucks",daf:"daf-trucks",db:"deutsche-bahn","sncf-voyageurs":"sncf",booking:"booking-com","google-shopping":"google"},X=e=>{const n=(e??"").trim().toLowerCase().replace(/_/g,"-");if(n){if(E(n))return n;if(R[n])return R[n];if(E(`${n}s`))return`${n}s`;if(n.endsWith("s")&&E(n.slice(0,-1)))return n.slice(0,-1)}},Q=()=>[...new Set(F.flatMap(e=>e.companies||[]).map(e=>e.trim()).filter(Boolean))].sort((e,n)=>e.localeCompare(n));export{F as C,Z as a,Y as b,Q as c,E as g,X as r};
